the g20 summit of the 20 wealthiest  nations and  emerging powers is hosted by saudi  arabia this year  but the country has repeatedly faced  international criticism  over human rights concerns well ahead of  the primarily  online forum members of the european  parliament urged  commission president ursula fonder lion  and the president of the european  council charles michelle  to boycott the event  jamal khashoggi an outspoken critic of  saudi arabia's government  murdered in a saudi consulate blog  arrived badawi publicly flocked and  sentenced to 10 years in prison for  allegedly insulting islam  activist lujina lahatrol detained for  seeking the right to  drive those are just three examples of  human rights violations in saudi arabia  the country hosting this year's g20  summit  since assuming the g20 presidency the  government of saudi arabia has been hard  at work  trying to rebrand its image so there's a  large global  public relations campaign underway in  order to encourage investment  in the country and yet behind these  initiatives and reform the reality on  the ground is that there is a brutal  crackdown  being led by the saudi authorities  against its own nationals  that's the reason why european lawmakers  are taking a stand  the eu cannot remain silent last month  they passed a resolution calling on eu  officials to downgrade their  participation in the summit even though  it's only a virtual event  mark tarabella is spearheading the  campaign we ask for the downgrading why  because an empty seat is not a good  policy  it would be better to send not a high  delegation  level but a delegation who  is able to deliver a message a strong  message toward the saudi authorities  the lawmakers also sent a personal  letter to european commission president  ozola fonder lion and european council  president  xil michelle we asked the commission for  their response and were told the summit  was too important  not to attend we've been raising our  concerns and we discussed further  how to improve the human rights  situation the protection of human rights  in the kingdom  and we will continue to pay utmost  attention to this question  eu officials say it's important to  maintain dialogue  and keep communication channels open but  human rights groups argue  leaders must use this summit as an  opportunity to ramp up the pressure on  riyadh  and hold the country to account for  human rights abuses  they demand that at least all the  women's rights activists currently in  jail should be released as soon as  possible  after all one of the themes of the g20  is women's empowerment  for more let's ask christian bloot who  specialises in international trade with  the bertelsmann institute  here in germany christian welcome saudi  arabia is hosting this weekend's g20  summit  the oil wealthy kingdom though was  recently and repeatedly  accused of major human rights violations  are they the right host  well whether or not they are the right  host um  they are probably not but what's  actually important is that the  international policy response to covet  has been lagging  behind the challenge for quite some time  now and it's really important that the  international community gets its act  together  and steps up the measures that are taken  against the pandemic and its economic  fallout  so i'd say it's very important to talk  even if this meeting is hosted by a  somewhat unsavory host  christian the summit starts as you  mentioned as we hit a critical phase  of the kovit 19 pandemic all of these  economies represented  need the vaccine to bounce back how much  will competition  for access to the vaccine be part of  these discussions  what's really important is that we find  a strategy to distribute  the vaccine worldwide if we don't manage  to do this  the covet virus can come back from a  remote corner of the world and will  haunt us again  so it's really important that we find a  way to coordinate  the distribution of vaccines that we do  so effectively  and that the financing gap which  currently still persists  is closed um i guess there will probably  an element of competition who gets it  first  which will be unavoidable but what's  really important is that eventually  everyone gets it christian from  kovid to the great unknown of this forum  this time around  this is likely donald trump's last  appearance on the world  stage as u.s president what are the  other nations expecting  from the outgoing president  well although this is a really important  meeting i think  expectations are very low and that has  to do with donald trump  his america first approach has turned  out to be extremely short-sighted  it hasn't served america well and it  hasn't served the international  community well  when i said that the international  policy response has really been lagging  behind the challenge what i meant is  we should have acted much earlier than  we have and offered that relief to poor  countries  rich countries have thrown an enormous  amount of money  at combatting covict and poor countries  haven't been able to do that in the same  degree  so donald trump will probably go down as  a president that has  hampered an effective response to this  massive crisis and everyone i think is  waiting for joe biden  to assume the presidency and to see more  leadership  from the united states in combating this  crisis  and others christian blood project  manager for the  bertelsmann foundation thank you so much  thank you  you
 

